Below is a ready‑to‑paste JavaScript snippet that shows how to invoke the **ChartSeriesGenerator** helper you described.  
Just replace the placeholder values (`axisLabelsArray`, `dataPointsArray`, `defaultColor`, `chartLayoutObject`) with your real data before running it.

```javascript
/* ------------------------------------------------------------------
   1️⃣  Define your data (replace the examples below with your own)
------------------------------------------------------------------- */
const axisLabelsArray   = ['January', 'February', 'March'];          // <-- your X‑axis labels
const dataPointsArray   = [12, 25, 18];                              // <-- your Y‑axis values
const defaultColor      = '#3498db';                                 // <-- your preferred color
const chartLayoutObject = {
  title: 'Monthly Sales',
  xAxis: { label: 'Month' },
  yAxis: { label: 'Revenue ($)' }
  // ... any other layout options you need
};

/* ------------------------------------------------------------------
   2️⃣  Call the helper – this creates the series and attaches it
------------------------------------------------------------------- */
const newSeries = ChartSeriesGenerator({
  labels: JSON.stringify(axisLabelsArray),          // Convert array to JS string
  data:   JSON.stringify(dataPointsArray),          // Convert array to JS string
  color:  defaultColor,                            // Optional – defaults if omitted
  chartLayout: JSON.stringify(chartLayoutObject)    // Convert layout object to JS string
});

/* ------------------------------------------------------------------
   3️⃣  Inspect / use the returned series
------------------------------------------------------------------- */
console.log('New series created:', newSeries);
// You can now add `newSeries` to your chart rendering logic, e.g.:
// chart.addSeries(newSeries);
```

### What this does

| Step | What happens |
|------|--------------|
| **1️⃣** | You supply the raw data: labels, numeric points, a color, and the chart’s layout configuration. |
| **2️⃣** | The helper serialises the arrays/objects into string representations (as required by the tool signature) and passes them to `ChartSeriesGenerator`. The helper then creates a new `ChartSeries` instance and attaches it to the supplied layout. |
| **3️⃣** | The helper returns the newly created series object, which you can log, store, or feed into your chart‑rendering pipeline. |

> **Tip:** If you’re working in a Node.js environment or a browser console, simply paste the snippet above after defining your real data. The helper will return the series object immediately, so you can inspect it or use it right away.

